<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichen Itza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600–<a href="/years/1100">1100</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Chichén Itzá, Chichʼen Itzaʼ, Uuc Yabnal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chichen Itza was a large pre-Columbian city built by the Maya people, whose ruins lie in Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and Mérida. It was a major focal point of the Northern Maya Lowlands from the Late Classic into the early Postclassic, and in the 10th century rose to be a regional capital controlling the area from central Yucatán to the north coast. Its buildings mix central Mexican styles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenes">Chenes</a> styles of the region, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-chichen-itza">Temple of Kukulcán</a> (El Castillo), the Great Ball Court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a>. It declined as a regional center by 1100, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a>, who tried to make it a Spanish capital, abandoned it in 1534.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichen-itza.jpg" alt="Chichen Itza" width="330" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichen_Itza_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
